Exercise 2: Nmap Service-Version Detection

Objective

Identify the service and software version exposed by my Ubuntu web server using Nmap from Kali Linux.

Lab Environment

* Testing machine: Kali Linux
* Target machine: Ubuntu Linux
* Target IP: 192.168.56.2
* Tool: Nmap 7.94SVN
* Target service: Apache2 web server

Command Used

nmap -sV 192.168.56.2

Results

Port	State	Service	Detected version
80/tcp	Open	HTTP	Apache httpd 2.4.66 (Ubuntu)

Nmap reported that the host was up and that 999 of the 1,000 commonly scanned TCP ports were closed.

Analysis

Nmap identified an HTTP service on TCP port 80 and fingerprinted it as Apache httpd 2.4.66 (Ubuntu).

This is consistent with the Apache2 web server installed on my Ubuntu virtual machine. The result demonstrates how service-version detection provides more information than a basic port scan.

Version detection is an estimate based on network responses. Further verification on the target machine would be needed to confirm the installed package version.

What I Learned

* How to use the Nmap -sV option.
* How to identify services exposed on open ports.
* How service fingerprints can help with security assessment.
* Why software versions should be checked for available security updates.

Next Steps

